---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import CtaBand from '../../components/CtaBand.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Custom websites & web apps"
  description="Custom-built websites and web apps for service businesses: fast, plainly written, delivered in days to weeks at a fixed price. No templates, no page builders."
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <span class="eyebrow">Services / Websites &amp; web apps</span>
      <h1>A site built around the jobs you want more of.</h1>
      <p class="lede" style="margin-top: var(--space-4);">
        Not a template with your logo dropped in. A custom build that says
        what you do, proves you're legitimate, and makes contacting you the
        easiest thing on the page.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--rule">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">Who this is for</span>
        <h2>Service businesses that live on inbound calls.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          Contractors, trades, clinics, studios, firms — any business where a
          person finds you, sizes you up in ten seconds, and decides whether
          to call. If that describes your customers, this is built for you.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">Websites</span>
        <h2>What every site includes</h2>
        <p class="muted">
          The same checklist ships with every build, one-pager or full site.
          Nothing on it is an upsell.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid--2">
        <ul class="checklist">
          <li>Custom design — no themes, no page builders, no template smell</li>
          <li>Copywriting included: you talk, I write, you approve</li>
          <li>Click-to-call buttons and short forms, placed where thumbs go</li>
          <li>Mobile-first build, tested on real phones before launch</li>
          <li>Fast static pages — no plugins to update, very little to hack</li>
        </ul>
        <ul class="checklist">
          <li>On-page SEO groundwork: titles, descriptions, structured data, sitemap</li>
          <li>Google Business Profile checklist so your listing matches your site</li>
          <li>Hosting set up in your accounts, often at $0/month</li>
          <li>Redirects from your old site so existing links keep working</li>
          <li>A recorded walkthrough and a plain-English owner's guide</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--rule">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">Web apps</span>
        <h2>Software for the part of the business that outgrew spreadsheets.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          Booking flows, quote calculators, job tracking, client portals —
          the tools that run your operation. If your process lives in a
          spreadsheet held together with habit, it can usually be a web app
          your whole crew can use from a phone.
        </p>
      </div>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>Scoped in writing first — a spec you approve before the quote</li>
        <li>Fixed price against that spec, not an hourly meter</li>
        <li>Built custom, so there's no per-seat license bill forever</li>
        <li>Your data, your accounts, your code — from day one</li>
        <li>Training for your staff, recorded so new hires can watch it later</li>
        <li>30 days of post-launch fixes included, no invoice</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--rule">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">How it's built</span>
        <h2>Modern tooling is the whole trick.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          I build with AI-accelerated tools and I'll never pretend otherwise.
          It's how one person designs, writes, and ships agency-quality work
          in days — and why the price is lower than a shop carrying six
          salaries. The judgment, the taste, and the accountability are human.
          Mine, specifically.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid--3">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Fast by default</h3>
          <p class="muted">
            Static pages, no bloated plugins, no five-megabyte sliders. Speed
            is a design decision, not an add-on.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Nothing to babysit</h3>
          <p class="muted">
            No CMS to patch, no plugin updates breaking the site on a
            Saturday. Edits go through me in days, or through the optional
            care plan.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Yours, completely</h3>
          <p class="muted">
            Domain, hosting, and code live in your accounts. If we part ways,
            you lose nothing. That's on purpose.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--rule">
    <div class="container container--narrow">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">Questions</span>
        <h2>Asked often, answered straight.</h2>
      </div>
      <details class="faq">
        <summary>How long does it take?</summary>
        <div class="faq-body">
          <p>
            One-page sites ship in about five business days from kickoff. Full
            business sites take two to three weeks. Web apps are scoped per
            project; most ship in three to six weeks. Your quote includes a
            delivery date, and the date is part of the deal.
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details class="faq">
        <summary>Will my site show up on Google?</summary>
        <div class="faq-body">
          <p>
            Every site ships with the technical groundwork search engines
            read: clean structure, fast loads, titles, structured data, a
            sitemap. What I won't do is promise you a ranking — nobody
            controls Google, and anyone promising a spot on page one is
            selling something else.
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details class="faq">
        <summary>Do you use templates?</summary>
        <div class="faq-body">
          <p>
            No. Every site is designed and written for the business it
            belongs to. Templates are why every plumber's website looks like
            every other plumber's website.
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details class="faq">
        <summary>What if I already have a site?</summary>
        <div class="faq-body">
          <p>
            I'll start with a recorded video audit of what you have — what's
            working, what's costing you calls, and whether you need a rebuild
            or a repair. If a repair is the honest answer, that's the answer
            you'll get.
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details class="faq">
        <summary>Do we need to meet in person?</summary>
        <div class="faq-body">
          <p>
            No. Everything is delivered remotely — video audits, video calls,
            and async review links you can open whenever you have ten minutes.
            I'm based in Los Angeles and work with businesses nationwide.
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CtaBand />
</Base>
